---
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import { homeSchema } from '../data/demos';
import Opener from '../components/sections/Opener.astro';
import LeadOffer from '../components/sections/LeadOffer.astro';
import CheckBand from '../components/sections/CheckBand.astro';
import Objection from '../components/sections/Objection.astro';
import AssessmentBand from '../components/sections/AssessmentBand.astro';
import Breadth from '../components/sections/Breadth.astro';
import ToolsDoorway from '../components/sections/ToolsDoorway.astro';
import Cta from '../components/sections/Cta.astro';
import { shapesHead } from '../data/home';

const origin = new URL(Astro.site!).origin;
---
<MarketingLayout
  title="AppliedIQ Solutions"
  description="Custom software for small local business: a real website you own, your spreadsheets turned into a live tool, and finished tools that run in your own browser. Yours to own, not rented."
  schema={homeSchema(origin)}
>
  {/* THE PITCH, not a navigation hub (D29). A visitor arriving from Google, a
      business card, or Nextdoor lands on the offer — not on a menu asking them
      to pick a door.

      ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      THIS PAGE'S JOB IS GETTING A HAND ON A TOOL. Operator's answer 2026-09-11
      on a fork put to him explicitly: D36 says the shelf is the offer, D34 says
      the free check is the lead offer, both cannot lead. He chose the tool.

      ── D113: THE SHAPE, 2026-09-12 ───────────────────────────────────────────

      He compared five treatments of this same copy and chose the combined one.
      What changed is the SHAPE of the bands and the ORDER they run in. What did
      NOT change is the palette, the typefaces, the motif or the section
      spacing — he ruled all four out, and spacing he ruled explicitly fine.

      THE FIX IS THIS REPO'S OWN PRECEDENT. src/components/kit/industrial.js,
      under WHY THREE AND NOT SEVEN, records nine shelf tools distinguished by
      accent hue reading as "still copies of one thing with the color changed",
      and being fixed by varying GROUND LIGHTNESS, GROUND WARMTH, TYPE PAIRING,
      DENSITY and RULE WEIGHT — "a hue is the lightest signal on a page". These
      pages varied none of the five. Now they do:

        Opener      canvas · widest column · display XL · loose
        LeadOffer   surface · standard · the ask on a brass rule
        CheckBand   DEEP ground · TIGHT · a 3px rule and tabular figures
        Objection   canvas · POPOUT column · type-led, no eyebrow, no numeral
        Assessment  surface · the split row, now the ONLY band using it
        Breadth     INSTRUMENT — the dark chassis, read from industrial.js
        Cta         canvas · centered · narrow

      TWO BANDS USED TO BE THE SAME MARKUP WITH DIFFERENT WORDS. CheckBand and
      AssessmentBand were identical split rows sitting back to back, each
      leaving its middle third empty. That pair was most of the complaint.

      THE ORDER ANSWERS THE COMPETITIVE SWEEP. Every rival in these counties
      asks to be believed — logos, testimonials, counts, one site's counts
      contradicting each other. This practice can ask to be TESTED, and nobody
      local or national claims to have personally run the operation the software
      is for. Those two are the unclaimed ground and they are now the first
      screen: the run of trades on the left, a finished tool on the right.

      THE SIX-TRADE HEADLINE STAYED, against the mockup he approved, which had
      demoted it. D33 is the positioning and PICKUP records twice that he
      declined reopening that headline; approving a whole-page mockup is not the
      same act. Opener.astro carries the full reasoning and the size change that
      solved the phone problem instead.

      TWO PLACEHOLDERS ARE IN HERE AND BOTH ARE MARKED AT THEIR SOURCE: the
      photograph is a 2020 capture he took knowingly as a stand-in, and the
      Objection band's copy was drafted by Claude and he has not passed it. See
      home.ts's `objection` comment. Neither is settled.

      NO SECTION COUNT IS WRITTEN HERE, and the last one was wrong.
      `node scripts/print-home-order.mjs` prints what the built page actually
      paints, heading by heading. Read it before writing anything that describes
      this page: a component name can outlive its content, and one of the names
      below already has.

      Copy lives in src/data/home.ts. */}
  <Opener />

  {/* `LeadOffer` IS THE TOOLS BAND. The export name belongs to the previous
      occupant; home.ts's THE EXPORT NAME STAYS comment records why keeping it
      was right for the code and wrong for a reader. It carries the screenshot
      on a PHONE only — the Opener has it on desktop — so the shot renders
      exactly once at every width. */}
  <LeadOffer />

  {/* Still the lead offer, still free, still ungated, still the only thing here
      a stranger with no software can act on (D34 untouched). It is now set as
      the arithmetic it sells rather than as one more paragraph with a button. */}
  <CheckBand />

  {/* The objection band. The one sentence on the page that could lose a
      customer, which is the move every well-made site in the research shares
      and the one thing a template cannot generate. */}
  <Objection />

  {/* The assessment sits below the free things and nowhere higher. A paid item
      above the only thing a stranger can act on pulls the reader out of the
      funnel at the point it is meant to draw them in. It names no figure, which
      keeps it one band tall — the price and the credit are one statement and
      verify-assessment.mjs requires them within 400 characters of each other. */}
  <AssessmentBand />

  {/* PROOF AT SCALE, ON THE DARK CHASSIS. Dark already means industrial here
      and light already means yours (themes.js), so this band is the hard cut
      and the cut is the argument. No new color: industrial.js's values are
      measured at 81 pairs against the WCAG floor and re-checked on every build.
      No numeral is passed — see Breadth.astro. */}
  <Breadth
    id="shapes"
    tone="instrument"
    index=""
    eyebrow="Proven at scale · the same arithmetic"
    title={shapesHead.title}
    lede={shapesHead.lede}
    columns={2}
    showAreas={false}
    showScales={true}
  >
    <ToolsDoorway />
  </Breadth>

  <Cta />
</MarketingLayout>
